## Overview

Live Audio Rooms is a real-time, many-to-many audio conversation product (Clubhouse/Twitter Spaces style) where a small set of speakers broadcast to a large set of listeners with low latency, while moderators can instantly control who is allowed to speak and what the audience hears.

The key insight is to **separate the system into a strict control plane and a fast media plane**, and to **avoid server-side mixing for the live path**. Use an SFU to forward a small number of speaker streams, let clients mix locally, and enforce moderation at the SFU so “mute/kick” is immediate even if the speaker’s client misbehaves.

This design stays boring where possible (WebRTC + SFU + Postgres + Kafka), and spends complexity only on the two hard parts: **scalable fan-out** and **authoritative moderation**.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive implementations usually fall into one of two traps:

